## Root cause

The outage was caused by a duplicate ModSecurity rule ID being loaded into the ingress controller configuration.

The following error was observed in the ModSecurity ingress controller logs shortly after the mitigation rollout:

```text
Error: exit status 1
2026/11/25 17:03:28 [notice] 365#365: ModSecurity-nginx v1.0.4 (rules loaded inline/local/remote: 2059/840/0)
2026/11/25 17:03:28 [notice] 365#365: libmodsecurity3 version 3.0.17
2026/11/25 17:03:28 [emerg] 365#365: "return" directive Rule id: 1100101 is duplicated
 in /tmp/nginx/nginx-cfg1376883494:484833
nginx: [emerg] "return" directive Rule id: 1100101 is duplicated
 in /tmp/nginx/nginx-cfg1376883494:484833
nginx: configuration file /tmp/nginx/nginx-cfg1376883494 test failed
```

The duplicate rule prevented NGINX from successfully validating and loading its configuration, causing ingress controller failures.

The possibility of duplicate rule IDs had been considered during development of the mitigation. However, validation focused on the Cloud Platform-managed ModSecurity configuration and did not account for rules defined by application teams.

Following investigation, the duplicate rule ID (`1100101`) was found to exist in application-managed ingress configuration.

The rule ID was subsequently identified in [laa-record-controlled-work-api staging values](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/laa-record-controlled-work-api/blob/main/helm/values/staging.yml). Although the rule appears commented out in the repository, the same rule ID was present in a live ingress configuration.

The duplicate could be identified in the cluster using:

```bash
kubectl get configmap -A -o json | jq -r '.items[] | select(tostring | contains("1100101")) | "\(.metadata.namespace)/\(.metadata.name)"'
```

## Production-specific behaviour

During the investigation there was initially some confusion around why the corrected mitigation could be successfully deployed to non-production ingress controllers but the original deployment had failed.

The root cause was that the conflicting ModSecurity rule only existed on a production ingress controller. As a result:

- The duplicate rule ID was not encountered during deployment to non-production ingress controllers.
- The duplicate rule ID only manifested when the mitigation was applied to production ingress controllers.

## Certificate Errors

During the incident, users reported certificate warnings when accessing services, and the Cloud Platform team observed the following message in ingress controller logs:

```text
Hostname/IP does not match certificate's altnames: Host: sign-in.hmpps.service.justice.gov.uk. is not in the cert's altnames: DNS:*.apps.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk, DNS:*.apps.live-1.cloud-platform.service.justice.gov.uk, DNS:*.apps.live.c...
```

Initially this appeared related to the outage because users were also reporting certificate errors.

Further investigation determined that:

- The log message was present before the mitigation rollout.
- The log message continued after rollback and successful deployment of the corrected mitigation.
- There was no evidence linking this message to the outage.

The message is therefore considered unrelated to the incident despite appearing consistent with the symptoms being reported.

## Resolution
  The initial mitigation was rolled back. Investigation identified a duplicate ModSecurity rule ID conflict causing ingress controller configuration validation failures.

  A corrected version of the mitigation using a non-conflicting rule ID was deployed to non-production ingress controllers before being promoted to production ingress controllers. Following deployment of the corrected mitigation, service was restored.

## Review actions

  1. Investigate implementation of automated detection of duplicate ModSecurity rule IDs in Cloud Platform CI/CD pipelines.
  2. Investigate integration testing that validates the rendered ingress configuration prior to deployment.
  3. Validate rule uniqueness independently for production and non-production ingress configurations.
  4. Review why users are not using non-prod modsec controllers, encourage use where possible. Note we won't enforce this as users need flexibility in choosing which modsec controller to use. 
  5. Investigate the certificate-altname ingress log message and determine whether it should be suppressed, reduced in verbosity, or documented as an expected benign condition.
  6. Document lessons learned and validation checks for future emergency security mitigation deployments.
  7. Ensure future service-impacting events are formally declared as incidents. Although an incident was not declared on this occasion, the event met the definition of an incident because end-users were unable to access services.
  8. Investigate why test clusters hang on creation
  9. Consider archiving [moj-prototype-template](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/moj-prototype-template/) repository.
